00:00:12.979 --> 00:00:18.143
printing receipts with React and creating POS with JavaScript

00:00:23.375 --> 00:00:25.784
Let me introduce myself first

00:00:25.785 --> 00:00:29.905
I am a developer who creates value using web technology

00:00:31.049 --> 00:00:35.369
If there is a function that I need, I like to create it myself and release it as an open source

00:00:36.245 --> 00:00:44.099
These days, I'm interested in the Rust language with this cute mascot, so I'm trying to use it well

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.136
I'm currently working as a front-end developer at Toss Place

00:00:49.137 --> 00:00:53.014
You may be a little unfamiliar with Toss Place

00:00:53.015 --> 00:01:00.385
Toss Place is a company that has created services for business owners in the offline payment market

00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:05.897
What I want to talk about today is this receipt

00:01:06.885 --> 00:01:11.724
You've probably paid for a service at a store

00:01:11.725 --> 00:01:15.102
and received a receipt that looks like this

00:01:16.005 --> 00:01:19.540
I printed out this receipt through JavaScript

00:01:19.541 --> 00:01:21.110
How did I do this?

00:01:23.055 --> 00:01:30.221
Like the code on the right, I wrote the receipt output code using React

00:01:30.222 --> 00:01:33.758
Today I would like to share

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:37.853
with you my experience of what kind of problem I had

00:01:37.854 --> 00:01:40.889
and how I ended up writing this code using React

00:01:43.065 --> 00:01:45.911
One day I made POS

00:01:45.912 --> 00:01:48.707
Does anyone know what POS is?

00:01:50.158 --> 00:01:51.947
There are a few

00:01:51.948 --> 00:01:58.868
If you have experience working in a convenience store or restaurant, you have probably used it at least once

00:01:59.154 --> 00:02:01.562
POS refers to a device that looks like this

00:02:02.701 --> 00:02:08.663
POS is a system that handles various financial and management tasks required for store operation

00:02:08.666 --> 00:02:11.730
They say it's POS, both hardware and software

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:18.286
For example, when we go to a restaurant, we order food,

00:02:18.287 --> 00:02:21.764
we enjoy the food, and we pay when we leave, right?

00:02:21.765 --> 00:02:26.474
When you stand in front of the cashier and the owner asks for a card, you give it to him or her

00:02:26.475 --> 00:02:31.091
There is a machine that looks a little big, put a card on it,

00:02:31.092 --> 00:02:36.340
and after paying, click on the screen and the payment is completed,

00:02:37.662 --> 00:02:40.067
the receipt is printed, and we receive the receipt

00:02:40.068 --> 00:02:44.805
In the process, the device in front of the owner is a POS device

00:02:44.806 --> 00:02:50.693
As you can see, the POS machine has a lot of complex UIs on the touchable display

00:02:53.321 --> 00:02:57.494
The POS machine manages a lot of things, such as orders and payments,

00:02:57.495 --> 00:03:00.776
so there are a lot of different things to print

00:03:00.777 --> 00:03:04.534
Since I am a consumer, I only knew about the receipt

00:03:06.159 --> 00:03:11.710
But there are orders that you can use in the kitchen

00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:15.080
Receipts that we are familiar with

00:03:15.081 --> 00:03:21.600
or bills going out to the customer's table were also printed

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:28.490
These receipts, bills, and orders are very small compared to the A4 paper we usually print

00:03:28.491 --> 00:03:33.100
When you touch the paper, you can get a slightly different texture

00:03:33.101 --> 00:03:35.689
This is because it is made of special paper

00:03:37.381 --> 00:03:40.298
The store mainly uses the printer

00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:44.190
called Thermal Printer in English

00:03:44.191 --> 00:03:48.630
The thermal printer is a method of printing paper by applying heat to it

00:03:51.281 --> 00:03:56.074
So when you touch the receipt in the store, you know that the material is very special

00:03:56.075 --> 00:04:02.958
Because this thermal printer uses special paper, the maintenance cost is a bit high

00:04:02.959 --> 00:04:06.406
It prints very quickly and does not require ink,

00:04:06.407 --> 00:04:10.998
so it is mainly used as the preferred printer in stores

00:04:10.999 --> 00:04:14.492
You'll hear the word printer a lot in future presentations

00:04:14.493 --> 00:04:18.345
I think you can think of this thermal printer as the printer here

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:27.787
If you look at the back of the thermal printer, it is usually made up as shown in the figure

00:04:28.681 --> 00:04:30.650
This is the power supply

00:04:30.651 --> 00:04:34.330
This is LAN line, when communicating over LAN

00:04:34.331 --> 00:04:36.060
and USB flash drive

00:04:36.061 --> 00:04:39.260
And finally, there's the serial port

00:04:39.261 --> 00:04:43.588
Some of the more expensive printers support Bluetooth as well

00:04:44.521 --> 00:04:49.906
In the store, it is said that the POS device is mainly connected to the serial port

00:04:51.287 --> 00:04:53.250
What on earth is the serial port anyway?

00:04:53.251 --> 00:04:55.868
It was the first word I had ever heard in my life

00:04:55.869 --> 00:04:57.836
I know the cereal we eat

00:04:59.181 --> 00:05:00.976
I've never heard of the serial port

00:05:02.321 --> 00:05:07.663
Serial port literally means serial port

00:05:07.664 --> 00:05:10.526
It is one of the physical interfaces of serial communication

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:15.579
Serial communication literally refers to a method of communicating serially

00:05:16.471 --> 00:05:22.178
USB flash drive, DisplayPort, Thunderbolt, Ethernet, etc. are all serial communication

00:05:23.571 --> 00:05:28.631
Even USB stands for Universal Serial Bus

00:05:28.632 --> 00:05:31.243
As the name suggests, the meaning of serial is included, right?

00:05:32.481 --> 00:05:37.626
A serial port usually refers to hardware that conforms to a standard called RS232

00:05:41.337 --> 00:05:43.656
Normal speed is

00:05:43.657 --> 00:05:48.960
For example, the most commonly used speed is 115200 bps

00:05:48.961 --> 00:05:52.846
bps is the number of bits sent per second

00:05:53.687 --> 00:05:59.026
115200 bps is 11.52 KB per second

00:05:59.027 --> 00:06:05.615
USB 2.0 can theoretically transfer about 60MB per second

00:06:05.953 --> 00:06:10.066
The serial port is very slow

00:06:10.067 --> 00:06:16.159
But the serial port is strong against noise and the interface is simple, so it is easy to connect

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.745
So, there are still a lot of serial ports in use in industrial sites

00:06:21.937 --> 00:06:25.687
The physical interface standard that looks like this is RS-232

00:06:26.317 --> 00:06:32.516
If you look at your old computer, you will sometimes see these standards

00:06:32.517 --> 00:06:35.835
This model was introduced in the 1960s

00:06:35.836 --> 00:06:41.158
In the past, it was used to connect peripheral devices such as a mouse, keyboard, or modem

00:06:43.301 --> 00:06:48.007
I'll show you how to control the serial port with JavaScript

00:06:48.897 --> 00:06:54.295
There's a way to use it as Node. There's a Node Serial Port library

00:06:54.296 --> 00:06:56.931
This is a third party library

00:06:56.932 --> 00:07:01.331
It supports Linux, MacOS, Windows, and various platforms

00:07:02.527 --> 00:07:05.646
It is provided through the Node Stream API

00:07:05.647 --> 00:07:08.488
There is even a method available on the web

00:07:09.287 --> 00:07:13.482
There is something called Web Serial API that is provided experimentally

00:07:15.277 --> 00:07:19.861
Web Serial API is an API that can handle serial ports on the web

00:07:20.947 --> 00:07:24.506
Input and output can be controlled as readable and writable, respectively.

00:07:24.507 --> 00:07:28.789
Each is provided through the Streams API

00:07:30.847 --> 00:07:33.247
Let's take a look at the Web Serial API

00:07:33.977 --> 00:07:40.706
The web serial API can be accessed as a property called serial in the navigator

00:07:40.707 --> 00:07:45.056
In the code here, you can select the port with requestPort

00:07:46.047 --> 00:07:49.996
And you can open a port using the open method

00:07:49.997 --> 00:07:54.916
Here, you need to enter a value called baudRate

00:07:54.917 --> 00:08:02.216
To put it simply, this baudRate can be thought of as the number of bits that are transmitted per modulation

00:08:02.217 --> 00:08:07.531
In the case of a serial port, it transmits once per modulation

00:08:07.532 --> 00:08:11.197
so you can think of 1baudRate as just the same as bps

00:08:12.247 --> 00:08:16.356
It means that the number of bits transmitted per second is 9600

00:08:16.357 --> 00:08:19.396
You must enter this baudRate

00:08:19.397 --> 00:08:24.683
This is because the baudRate is required for normal communication between the two devices

00:08:25.827 --> 00:08:28.431
Let me explain why

00:08:28.432 --> 00:08:33.453
For example, let's talk about when data is transmitted from transmitter to receiver

00:08:33.453 --> 00:08:36.890
This transmitter is Tx and receiver is Rx

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:40.870
Tx is sending and Rx is receiver

00:08:41.582 --> 00:08:45.832
To transmit data, the voltage signal difference is used

00:08:45.833 --> 00:08:50.282
For example, there is a positive voltage and a negative voltage

00:08:50.283 --> 00:08:54.619
In the case of a serial port, positive voltage is recognized as 0 and negative voltage is recognized as 1

00:08:56.233 --> 00:09:01.821
In this way, the signal can be moved, so let's convert it into a binary signal

00:09:03.883 --> 00:09:05.522
It can be interpreted this way

00:09:06.893 --> 00:09:08.396
But is this correct?

00:09:09.763 --> 00:09:14.012
Unfortunately, I can't say that it's perfectly correct

00:09:14.013 --> 00:09:15.866
For example, you can read it like this as well

00:09:16.937 --> 00:09:21.193
In other words, the data can be read accurately only if there is a transmission speed

00:09:21.294 --> 00:09:23.831
In order to communicate properly through the serial port,

00:09:23.832 --> 00:09:27.337
the transmission speed must be matched between the two devices

00:09:28.373 --> 00:09:32.213
You can come back and open it using baudRate

00:09:32.214 --> 00:09:40.426
You can read and write data through the open port with a readable or writable interface

00:09:41.323 --> 00:09:43.377
When writing data,

00:09:45.063 --> 00:09:51.747
there is something called Uint8Array, which is an interface for handling binary data in JavaScript

00:09:53.095 --> 00:09:55.798
If you haven't dealt with it, it might be a little unfamiliar

00:09:55.799 --> 00:10:01.881
JavaScript provides a TypedArray that can handle binary data

00:10:01.883 --> 00:10:06.879
One of these TypedArrays is Uint8Array

00:10:08.523 --> 00:10:12.972
Let me explain a bit more about Uint8Array

00:10:12.973 --> 00:10:20.119
For example, Uint8Array is an array of unsigned 8-bit integers per cell

00:10:21.003 --> 00:10:27.786
For example, if I put text ab in Uint8Array in ASCII encoding,

00:10:28.453 --> 00:10:33.646
The first cell will contain 0x61, the second cell will contain 0x62 like this

00:10:35.293 --> 00:10:40.642
If Ga ab is included in EUC-KR encoding,

00:10:40.643 --> 00:10:47.556
Ga will contain two cells like 0xB0 0xA1, and ab will be contained in the other two cells

00:10:48.563 --> 00:10:52.162
Likewise, if you encode once in UTF-8,

00:10:52.163 --> 00:10:55.282
In this way, you can check that it is put in five cells

00:10:55.283 --> 00:10:59.644
In this way, we can handle binary data using Uint8Array

00:11:01.383 --> 00:11:06.342
Next, I'll write the code that actually prints

00:11:06.343 --> 00:11:09.744
I'll bring the writable interface to the port here,

00:11:09.745 --> 00:11:15.170
encode the value of Hello World! as ASCII, and write it to the port

00:11:21.393 --> 00:11:23.132
Surprisingly, there's no response

00:11:24.388 --> 00:11:26.398
Is there something wrong with the printer?

00:11:27.359 --> 00:11:32.018
In fact, it continues to accumulate in the buffer until a print command is inputted to the printer

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:36.597
You have to enter the printer output command

00:11:36.598 --> 00:11:40.548
to realize that the printer has to print it out

00:11:40.549 --> 00:11:42.548
So what on earth is the output command?

00:11:45.399 --> 00:11:48.385
There is an output command called ESC/POS

00:11:48.859 --> 00:11:52.763
This command language is a kind of ESC/P

00:11:53.559 --> 00:11:57.818
ESC/P stands for Epson Standard Code for Printers

00:11:57.819 --> 00:12:00.090
It is one of the languages for controlling the printer

00:12:00.849 --> 00:12:06.810
The ESC/P command is usually used as an industry standard

00:12:08.369 --> 00:12:12.004
ESC/POS is a type of ESC/P

00:12:12.619 --> 00:12:16.903
It is a printer command system made by Epson

00:12:16.904 --> 00:12:21.866
It is a command language compatible with thermal printers mostly used in POS

00:12:24.509 --> 00:12:27.054
Let's take a look at the command example

00:12:28.409 --> 00:12:34.303
Line feed, ASCII code, that corresponds to LF, prints and publishes LF

00:12:34.304 --> 00:12:39.898
If you use ESC @, the printer is initialized

00:12:39.899 --> 00:12:42.438
There are several commands like this

00:12:42.439 --> 00:12:46.776
As you can see, ESC is mainly used as a prefix,

00:12:46.777 --> 00:12:50.143
so I think the first letter is named ESC

00:12:51.223 --> 00:12:53.624
In addition, there are various commands

00:12:53.625 --> 00:12:56.318
Then, the reason why 'Hello World!' was not outputted earlier is

00:12:56.319 --> 00:13:02.698
because there is no newline output, so I'll put a command that outputs this line and print it

00:13:02.699 --> 00:13:05.522
There's 'Hello World!' and after line feed,

00:13:05.523 --> 00:13:09.913
If you put a command to initialize the printer and print it out,

00:13:14.719 --> 00:13:17.620
you can see that 'Hello World!' is outputted like this

00:13:18.799 --> 00:13:21.924
We've done something easy, so let's try something a little more difficult

00:13:22.809 --> 00:13:25.598
For example, I'm going to print out Korean

00:13:25.599 --> 00:13:29.126
Unfortunately, the thermal printer does not support UTF-8

00:13:29.729 --> 00:13:34.759
Because most of the printers on the market are used stably,

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:37.146
it says that UTF-8 is not supported

00:13:37.147 --> 00:13:40.077
So I'm going to encode with EUC-KR

00:13:40.078 --> 00:13:43.181
In this case, there is a command called ESC R

00:13:43.182 --> 00:13:46.552
You can use this to specify a set of characters in your code page

00:13:46.553 --> 00:13:51.340
So, if you enter n=13, it will be encoded as EUC-KR

00:13:52.509 --> 00:13:56.554
Put the command corresponding to ESC R in the data

00:13:56.555 --> 00:14:01.384
If you encode 'Hello' in EUC-KR and then print it out,

00:14:04.689 --> 00:14:06.930
you can see that it is outputted like this

00:14:08.119 --> 00:14:10.777
In addition to this, there are several other commands

00:14:11.413 --> 00:14:14.704
You can cut the printing paper

00:14:14.705 --> 00:14:19.921
Or you can change the font size or make it bold

00:14:19.922 --> 00:14:24.512
You can change the alignment, change the size, print barcodes,

00:14:24.513 --> 00:14:27.397
or print QR codes

00:14:27.398 --> 00:14:29.749
By using these various commands,

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:35.022
you can print a printout that is close to the receipt you usually see at the store

00:14:35.023 --> 00:14:39.272
But the code that creates the output data is very messy, right?

00:14:39.273 --> 00:14:40.944
Let's simplify a bit more

00:14:41.953 --> 00:14:47.803
I was thinking about printing printer data easily by defining a class called Printer

00:14:49.313 --> 00:14:51.113
The printer objects hold data

00:14:54.153 --> 00:14:56.608
about the command internally

00:14:56.609 --> 00:15:00.856
If you open a new line, newLine

00:15:00.857 --> 00:15:05.047
If you want to output text, call text and enter the value of this data

00:15:05.048 --> 00:15:09.453
Finally, when we do getData, we will change this to Uint8Array

00:15:12.243 --> 00:15:17.383
Using this printer object makes it very easy to print out the printer

00:15:17.384 --> 00:15:23.492
If you make a printer object and designate the character set, or change the text size,

00:15:23.493 --> 00:15:29.463
change the align, print the text, and do getData, printer data is easily created

00:15:30.953 --> 00:15:33.638
This seems to be good enough to use

00:15:35.233 --> 00:15:40.144
In fact, most of the libraries that support ESC/POS commands I found were

00:15:40.144 --> 00:15:43.082
implemented with this interface

00:15:43.083 --> 00:15:46.615
But I thought it has to be improved a little

00:15:48.343 --> 00:15:52.982
I think you can think of the printed as a kind of UI

00:15:52.983 --> 00:15:58.332
For example, if you think of it as UI that is output to a printer rather than the browser's DOM,

00:15:58.333 --> 00:16:02.222
the existing printer is made with the code written on the left

00:16:02.223 --> 00:16:09.643
Creating DOM elements directly, setting properties, and cumbersome procedural code comes out

00:16:10.405 --> 00:16:16.407
On the other hand, the code on the right shows what you want to express in the UI at a glance, right?

00:16:18.293 --> 00:16:20.705
I feel so declarative

00:16:21.593 --> 00:16:26.510
The one on the left is a bit more imperative code and the one on the right is more declarative

00:16:27.052 --> 00:16:32.672
We usually prefer to do it declaratively when we create the UI on top of the web browser

00:16:33.623 --> 00:16:38.838
So, if you think that the receipt is a kind of UI that is expressed as a printer output,

00:16:39.353 --> 00:16:44.852
I thought it would be nice to express the data to be printed with a declarative syntax

00:16:44.853 --> 00:16:47.983
Even the code is a bit short

00:16:50.393 --> 00:16:54.482
I was using React in my project,

00:16:54.483 --> 00:17:00.090
so I thought that I could easily write receipt data declaratively using JSX syntax

00:17:03.566 --> 00:17:05.476
The way to use React is

00:17:05.477 --> 00:17:08.567
Let's say you have a receipt that looks like this

00:17:08.568 --> 00:17:14.883
There are left-aligned text, center-aligned text, images, and lines

00:17:14.884 --> 00:17:19.845
Divide each compartment

00:17:19.846 --> 00:17:24.374
For example, you could treat the entire receipt as a Printer component

00:17:25.366 --> 00:17:28.569
The left-aligned text is a Text component like this,

00:17:28.570 --> 00:17:34.615
Center-aligned text is in the state that the align property is given to the Text component

00:17:34.616 --> 00:17:37.985
It can be divided into Image and Line

00:17:37.986 --> 00:17:40.918
I think it can be expressed in code like this

00:17:41.586 --> 00:17:45.221
So, we've been able to create this JSX code,

00:17:45.222 --> 00:17:49.416
but how do we change it to Uint8Array?

00:17:50.776 --> 00:17:55.596
The cool thing about React is that you just declare how to draw the UI,

00:17:55.797 --> 00:18:02.715
and the renderer puts the appropriate data at the right time and renders it in a state that suits the environment

00:18:02.716 --> 00:18:08.785
For example, react-dom renders to DOM in the browser environment

00:18:08.786 --> 00:18:14.270
In the case of react-native, it renders with Native UI

00:18:15.486 --> 00:18:19.256
If you take advantage of this, you can render in a form that supports ESC/POS commands

00:18:19.257 --> 00:18:24.659
in a printer environment with a little customization on the renderer

00:18:26.485 --> 00:18:29.774
In other words, you can think of a renderer

00:18:29.775 --> 00:18:35.765
that takes a random React element and converts it to Uint8Array

00:18:35.766 --> 00:18:41.414
If you use the render function, you can change the receipt code you created earlier to Uint8Array

00:18:41.415 --> 00:18:48.687
in this way and write it to the port and print it

00:18:50.226 --> 00:18:54.033
Let me print out a complicated receipt in this way

00:18:55.356 --> 00:18:58.893
It's printing some text and barcodes

00:18:59.696 --> 00:19:00.816
Using React like this,

00:19:01.736 --> 00:19:06.435
you can write it in a declarative form like the code on the right

00:19:07.206 --> 00:19:10.230
And I thought it would be nice to be able to easily predict

00:19:10.233 --> 00:19:13.950
what will be printed on the left by looking at the code on the right

00:19:15.386 --> 00:19:17.835
How can we implement the render function?

00:19:19.256 --> 00:19:26.815
The printer output does not require a render function that is complex enough to require scheduling or readjustment

00:19:26.816 --> 00:19:30.035
I thought we could just go around the React child nodes

00:19:30.036 --> 00:19:36.176
and generate output commands for each node to output node

00:19:37.116 --> 00:19:44.763
So, we declare a function called print that generates an output command for each component

00:19:46.176 --> 00:19:50.460
And when the render function receives a React element as input,

00:19:50.461 --> 00:19:54.996
All you have to do is run this print function while traveling around each child node

00:19:54.997 --> 00:19:57.056
Then

00:19:58.740 --> 00:20:03.269
Since data must have been accumulated in the printer after going around,

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:09.608
just getData will return the receipt output data in the form we want

00:20:11.576 --> 00:20:13.835
The actual implementation is a little more complicated,

00:20:13.836 --> 00:20:17.039
but conceptually it can be seen like this

00:20:19.146 --> 00:20:23.885
There is one more good thing about writing receipts like this in React syntax

00:20:23.886 --> 00:20:28.069
That is, rendering is possible even in an environment with DOM

00:20:29.406 --> 00:20:31.539
For example, when drawing on the DOM,

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:36.265
you can draw in this way using the renderer provided by react-dom

00:20:36.266 --> 00:20:43.887
Or, if you need a printout,

00:20:43.888 --> 00:20:45.902
you can print the receipt like this by rendering it as it corresponds to the printout

00:20:47.406 --> 00:20:50.850
In other words, if you style it appropriately,

00:20:50.851 --> 00:20:55.739
you can easily create a preview of how the receipt will be printed in the browser

00:20:58.966 --> 00:21:06.147
I did some interesting work by customizing the renderer of React in this way

00:21:07.976 --> 00:21:13.025
There were actually several packages that were made by customizing the renderer

00:21:13.026 --> 00:21:15.677
Besides react-dom or react-native

00:21:15.678 --> 00:21:21.179
In the case of rendering using react components in the terminal environment, there was a library called ink

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:25.015
If you want to render PDF, react-pdf

00:21:25.016 --> 00:21:28.334
The one I introduced today is called react-thermal-printer

00:21:28.335 --> 00:21:32.329
I made it open source on npm and distributed it

00:21:33.426 --> 00:21:36.786
One of React's slogans, Learn Once, Write Anywhere,

00:21:36.787 --> 00:21:41.685
I think it was a very relatable experience

00:21:41.686 --> 00:21:44.376
This is what I wanted to share

